In this case, the induced subgraph with vertices $\{A_1,A_3\}$ cancels with the induced subgraphs with vertex $A_1$ and $A_3$. The corresponding entropic equality reads,

\begin{equation}
 S(A_1A_2A_3)-S(A_1A_2)-S(A_2A_3)+S(A_2)=0
\end{equation}
which is precisely strong subadditivity expected for strict area-law systems.

If we proceed with other subgraphs, we realize that any subgraphs with fewer than 2 edges reduce to triviality. Therefore for 3 vertex graphs which represent tripartite systems, the graph theory approach only induce the above 2 relations (up to permutation of vertex labels).

